<BaseLayout title="About" description="About Ken Miko — accounting information systems, data analytics, and project controls.">
	<HeroBanner slot="banner" title="A bit about me :)" subtitle="Come find out more than you probably needed to know." />
	<p>Hi, I'm Ken Miko, short for Kennedy Mikolaszuk-Sills. I'm a BI/data analyst who loves taking a mess and giving it order, financial and technical systems in particular.</p>
	<p>I've worked across local government, USPS, small businesses, IT, and retail, and I kept running into the same thing: the systems people struggle with usually aren't complicated on purpose. They're just built without much thought for the actual humans in the process. Most of what I do, across projects, communication, and documentation, is close that gap.</p>
	<h2>How I Work</h2>
	<p>Systems should make people's lives easier, and I build toward that every time. My two core tenets are documentation and communication. It's a focus I've taken far enough to speak on: I presented "Designing Reports for Strategic Communications" at the 2026 OpenGov conference, on building reports that actually get the point across. A report can be about anything, but why this one? What's on it, who's it for, what's the point? That thinking is also why I treat accessibility as the point rather than an afterthought: the work should function for the person on mobile, the person who prints everything, and the person who needs it clearer to read, not just the ideal case.</p>
	<p>The throughline is education. The best deliverable teaches you how to use it, so every project I finish ships with documentation. I start with the big picture, then disappear into the details: goals, owners, deadlines, next steps. And when a project doesn't have those, I'll build that structure myself. "I don't know how" has never been a stopping point; that's just research I haven't done yet.</p>
	<h2>What I work with:</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>BI &amp; visualization:</strong> Power BI, Tableau (dashboards, KPI reporting, data visualization)</li>
		<li><strong>Data &amp; querying:</strong> SQL, Excel, Access (modeling, transformation, cleansing, ETL)</li>
		<li><strong>Automation:</strong> Power Automate (workflow automation and reporting pipelines)</li>
		<li><strong>Programming:</strong> Python (data transformation and scripting)</li>
		<li><strong>Enterprise systems:</strong> (OpenGov EAM, Microsoft 365)</li>
		<li><strong>Design:</strong> Adobe Illustrator &amp; InDesign, Canva, Word (documentation and visual communication)</li>
		<li><strong>AI-assisted:</strong> Claude Code, Copilot (builds run on plans and structure I define, never sensitive data handed off blindly)</li>
	</ul>
	<h2>How I Live</h2>
	<p>Outside work, I'm a lifelong learner to a slightly unreasonable degree, the kind who falls into Wikipedia rabbit holes from food science to olfactic communication (neat stuff, actually). My biggest fear is running out of time to learn everything I want to. That's where the blog comes from: the only difference between screwing around and science is writing it down.</p>
	<p>I'm finishing a BS in Accounting Information Systems and Data Analytics at LSUA (May 2027), with an MS in Informatics planned after. Being a lifelong Louisianian shaped most of what I value, showing up for people and treating community as something you actively do. The rest of what keeps me going is simple: my husband, my two yappy dogs, my close-knit family, and a stubborn belief that we're better off lifting each other up than tearing each other down. 🦭</p>

